{"name":"Bobcat Whiskers","hex":"#eadfd0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bobcat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A North American wildcat (Lynx rufus), having tufted ears and a short tail."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A multi-purpose construction vehicle, akin to a smaller version of a front-end loader or a backhoe (backhoe loader), with a one-man caged control cabin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bobcat"},{"term":"whiskers","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of whisker","Faded creases around the crotch of blue jeans. Can be added artificially or created by daily wear over the lifetime of a garment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whiskers"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bobcat","blocks":[{"text":"The bobcat, also known as the wildcat, bay lynx, or red lynx, is one of the four extant species within the medium-sized wild cat genus Lynx. Native to North America, it ranges from southern Canada through most of the contiguous United States to Oaxaca in Mexico. It is listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List since 2002, due to its wide distribution and large population. Although it has been hunted extensively both for sport and fur, populations have proven stable, though declining in some areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg/330px-Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobcat","wikidata":"Q131907"},{"title":"Whiskers","blocks":[{"text":"Whiskers, also known as vibrissae, are specialized sensory hairs that help most species of mammals sense their environment. Whiskers function as highly sensitive feelers that help mammals navigate the world in many different ways, including the detection of air currents and physical objects to aid in navigation, hunting, and spatial awareness. Whiskers can trigger reflexes to protect the eyes. Whiskers also play a significant role in the communication between individual animals in different species of mammals. They convey an animal's emotional state, essentially sending a message to other members of their species about what emotion they are feeling at the time. Whisker movement can be thought of as a form of language that animals use to silently communicate with the other animals around them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Cat_whiskers_closeup.jpg/330px-Cat_whiskers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whiskers","wikidata":"Q913572"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_rufus.png/120px-Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_rufus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_rufus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_rufus.png/120px-Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_rufus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AErnest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_rufus.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_canadensis.png/120px-Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_canadensis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_canadensis.png/250px-Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_canadensis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_canadensis.png/120px-Ernest_Ingersoll_-_lynx_canadensis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut